 ### [Soft Rasterizer](6779-soft-rasterizer.md)
-Soft Rasterizer is a differentiable rendering method that replaces the deterministic, z-buffer-based triangle selection of traditional rasterization with a probabilistic aggregation approach. It computes pixel color as a weighted sum of contributions from all mesh triangles, including occluded ones, by modeling each triangle as a probabilistic cloud with a blur radius. This allows gradients to flow to blocked and distant triangle vertices, making it particularly suitable for geometry optimization tasks where other methods fail to propagate gradients through occlusions.
+Soft Rasterizer is a differentiable rendering method that replaces the deterministic z-buffer depth test with a probabilistic aggregation, treating each triangle as a probabilistic cloud to compute pixel color as a weighted sum of contributions from all mesh triangles, including hidden ones. It solves the problem of non-differentiable visibility discontinuities in standard rasterization, allowing gradients to flow to occluded and distant triangle vertices, which makes it particularly suitable for geometry optimization tasks like single-view mesh reconstruction. Unlike its siblings, it does not use z-culling; instead, it relies on a probability map derived from 2D screen-space distances and depth to fuse color contributions, ensuring that the entire image contributes to the gradient of every vertex.
 
 ### [RGBXY Derivatives with Optimal Transport (DROT)](8830-rgbxy-derivatives-with-optimal-transport-drot.md)
-RGBXY Derivatives with Optimal Transport (DROT) is a differentiable rendering method that associates each screen-space pixel with a visible 3D geometric point (point proxy) to compute 5D RGBXY derivatives, measuring how 3D RGB color and 2D projected screen-space position change with respect to scene parameters. It combines these dense derivatives with an optimal transport-based loss function to find pixel matching between rendered and target images, solving the problem of converging in inverse rendering when initial and target object locations are not close. Unlike traditional methods that rely on sparse, local per-pixel color derivatives and L2 loss, DROT captures global and long-range object motions, making it superior for optimizing global translations and rotations.
+RGBXY Derivatives with Optimal Transport (DROT) is a differentiable rendering method that associates each screen-space pixel with a visible 3D geometric point (point proxy) to compute 5D RGBXY derivatives, measuring how 3D RGB color and 2D projected screen-space position change with respect to scene parameters. It combines these dense derivatives with an optimal transport-based loss function to find pixel matching between rendered and target images, solving the convergence problem in inverse rendering when initial and target object locations are not close. Unlike traditional methods that rely on sparse, local per-pixel color derivatives and L2 loss, DROT captures global and long-range object motions, making it superior for optimizing global translations and rotations.
 
